--- body ---
## Context

`erg migrate` bypasses the 3-state divergence check. `migrate.go:261` calls
`installAssets(root, migrateAssetPaths, false, false)` — `refuseDiverged` is
false, so a divergent `AGENTS.md` is overwritten unconditionally.

This is **read in the code, not demonstrated**: a fixture with a local edit
survived `erg migrate` in testing because the run reported `already clean` and
never reached the asset step. Whether the path actually fires on a store that
genuinely needs migrating is the open question, and settling it is this
ticket's first action.

Split out of 0278 (now a tracker) because 0278's own Action 1 named this the
most dangerous of the four paths in the set and said it must be settled
*before* the rest of the hardening work, since the finding could change that
work's shape. `Blocked-by: 0279` — both this ticket and 0279 touch
`installAssets`/`manifest.go`-adjacent code, so this lands after the
direction-blind-upgrade fix rather than in parallel with it, to avoid two PRs
editing the same functions concurrently.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/migrate.go` — line 261, the `refuseDiverged=false` call
- `src/go/init.go` — `installAssets`, `migrateAssetPaths`

## Actions

1. Build a fixture store that genuinely needs migrating (not `already clean`),
   give it a locally-edited `AGENTS.md`, run `erg migrate`, and record what
   happens. This is the open empirical question 0278 named — settle it first.
2. If it overwrites: apply the same divergence check used elsewhere
   (`refuseDiverged=true`, or the equivalent), so `erg migrate` never silently
   destroys a local edit. If a forced overwrite is still wanted for the
   migrate path, it must announce what it replaced.
3. If it preserves: document why `refuseDiverged=false` is safe here (e.g. an
   earlier gate already screens for divergence before this call is reached),
   and commit the fixture as a regression test so the finding survives future
   refactors of `migrate.go`.

## Test

Red step, and it doubles as Action 1's investigation: a fixture store that
genuinely needs migrating, carrying a locally-edited `AGENTS.md`, run through
`erg migrate`. Assert on the local edit's survival either way — the assertion
itself is the finding, whichever way it comes out.

## Verification

- [ ] path D's actual behaviour (overwrite or preserve) is recorded in this
      ticket's log, with the transcript
- [ ] the fixture from Action 1 is a committed regression test, not a one-off
      manual run
- [ ] a local edit survives `erg migrate`, with or without `--force`
      documented as the only route that can replace it, and only with
      announcement

## Invariants

- No tracked file is overwritten by `migrate` without saying so (same
  invariant 0278 states for the whole asset machinery).
- A pristine current asset stays a silent no-op under `migrate`, same as under
  `init`.

## Exit criteria

- `erg migrate`'s behaviour on a genuinely-divergent store carrying a local
  edit is settled, recorded, and covered by a committed test
- If path D was dangerous, it no longer silently destroys a local edit

## Related

- 0278 — tracker; this is path D, split out under the monster-ticket checklist
- 0279 — lands first; this ticket is `Blocked-by` it to keep the two PRs off
  the same functions
- 0281 — the remainder of 0278's hardening; it is `Blocked-by` this ticket,
  since D's outcome may change its scope

## Imagine

**Premise check: LIVE.** `installAssets(root, migrateAssetPaths, false, false)`
is still literally the call at `src/go/migrate.go:261` — `refuseDiverged=false`,
confirmed by reading the current file, not memory of the split-off text.

**Action 1's open question is already answered by reading the code — no new
fixture needed.** `cmdMigrate` (migrate.go:80) does two independent things: it
walks `tickets/*.erg` files reporting `migrated`/`already clean` counts, and —
separately, gated only on `filepath.Base(dir) == "tickets"` — it calls
`migrateLayout(dir)` (migrate.go:264-266), which is what reaches the
`installAssets` call at line 261 (inside `migrateLayout`, not `cmdMigrate`).
Nothing about the per-ticket `already clean` tally gates `migrateLayout`: it
runs whenever the directory is literally named `tickets`, regardless of
whether any `.erg` file needed a rewrite. So the earlier fixture that "reported
already clean and never reached the asset step" did not demonstrate immunity —
it most likely used a directory not named `tickets`, or otherwise never called
`migrateLayout`. Read straight, the asset step is reached on *every*
`erg migrate tickets/` run, unconditionally of ticket-file cleanliness.
**Confidence: high**, from control flow alone (migrate.go:80-181 for the
per-file walk, 264-266 for the gate, 182-261 for `migrateLayout` itself — no
early return before line 261 depends on ticket-file state, only on
archive/closed collisions or self-copy I/O errors).

**Path D does fire on a diverged AGENTS.md, and it is proven — by an existing,
already-committed test, not a hypothetical.** `src/go/migrate_test.go:448`
(`TestMigrateLayoutErgrc`, subtest "diverged AGENTS.md is force-overwritten by
migrateLayout") writes `AGENTS.md = "DIVERGED\n"`, calls `migrateLayout`
directly, and asserts the content is replaced with the embedded asset —
i.e. it asserts the overwrite as the *wanted* outcome, not a regression to
guard against. `tests/test_migrate.sh:391-401` does the same at the
integration level (`$ERG migrate "$ADIR/tickets"` on a fresh dir with no
tickets needing migration, `DIVERGED` AGENTS.md, asserts it is gone
afterward). Both predate this ticket.

**This is not an open bug — it is a closed, deliberate, charter-level
decision.** `docs/erg-imagine-charter.md` (~line 124-127, French) states
migrate "reste en force-overwrite (la migration est une étape revue)" and is
"exempt du prompt dpkg". Ticket `tickets/closed/0224-...erg` (closed via PR
#275) is the paper trail: it originally proposed exactly what this ticket's
Action 2 proposes (`refuseDiverged=true` for migrate), a raid flag caught that
this reverses the documented charter decision, escalated to the author, and
the author's actual resolution was narrower — separation of concerns, not a
protection flag: `.ergrc` (configuration) was excluded from migrate's asset
list entirely (now delivered only by `erg init`'s dpkg 3-state path), while
`AGENTS.md` explicitly *keeps* force-overwrite because "agent docs track the
binary." The two tests cited above are 0224's own exit-criterion tests for
that decision, not incidental coverage.

**It is also not silent, contrary to this ticket's "silently destroys" framing
— though the label is wrong.** `installAssets` prints
`init: refreshed tickets/AGENTS.md (git restore -- tickets/AGENTS.md to undo)`
to stderr on every overwrite (init.go, end of the write branch), and
`migrateLayout` additionally prints a summary count
(`migrate: AGENTS.md refreshed (...)`). The per-file line is hardcoded
`"init: ..."` even when the caller is `migrate` — a real but cosmetic mislabel
(the doc comment on `installAssets` says callers get to label messages with
their own command name; this one line doesn't). Below the severity floor for
its own ticket; noting it here for the record rather than filing.

**Antipattern scan.** Action 2 as written (apply `refuseDiverged=true` to the
migrate call site) would silently reopen a decision the author already made
explicitly and narrowly in 0224, and would break the two existing tests cited
above (they assert the opposite). That is not YAGNI or premature abstraction
in the usual sense, but the same-shaped trap: rebuilding machinery (a
divergence check) that was deliberately *not* wanted at this call site, per a
documented decision this ticket's author already rendered. Action 3's framing
("if it preserves: document why, and commit a regression test") is structurally
the closer match to what's actually true here, except the fixture and the
documentation both already exist (0224 + the charter) — so even Action 3's
remaining work is mostly citation, not construction.

**Recommendation.** Downgrade this ticket's scope: no code change to
`installAssets`/`migrate.go` is indicated. The concrete remainder is (a)
record this finding in the log with a pointer to 0224 and the charter line,
satisfying the "settled, recorded, covered by a committed test" exit
criterion using the tests that already exist, and (b) optionally, as a
separate below-floor note (not a ticket per the severity floor: cosmetic,
non-destructive), the `"init:"` label on the per-file refresh message when
called from `migrate`. If the author wants to revisit the charter decision
itself, that's a new, explicit ticket against the charter/0224 outcome, not a
silent reversal riding in on this one.
